IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Writ Petition (Civil) No. 202 of 1995
CITATION: (1997) 2 SCC 267
YEAR: 1997

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: J.S. Verma, B.N. Kirpal

PARTIES:
T.N. Godavarman vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980, Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Article 48A, Article 51A(g)

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
dictionary meaning of forest, tree felling ban, continuing mandamus, environmental conservation

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Established landmark continuing mandamus defining forests by dictionary meaning across India, restricting non-forest activities without central environmental clearance.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Article 48A, Article 51A(g).

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

